WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.700
Let's dive into using some of the Sequencer tools

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.019
inside of the editor itself.

3
00:00:07.019 --> 00:00:09.140
So I'm going to leave this slide up for just a second

4
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.900
to briefly cover the main layout panels within Sequencer.

5
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.140
And it's important to note that you have to have a sequence open.

6
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.739
I'll show you here in just a second

7
00:00:18.739 --> 00:00:21.140
if we don't have an active sequence, how it

8
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.739
will look like the tool is broken.

9
00:00:22.739 --> 00:00:23.300
It's not.

10
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.660
We simply just need to open a sequence,

11
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.739
and we'll have all these at our disposal.

12
00:00:27.739 --> 00:00:30.780
So kind of moving across the top from left to right,

13
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.240
so we have our main toolbar.

14
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.899
We have various different features on here

15
00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:35.460
that we'll cover in a second.

16
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.340
In the second panel over here, we actually have our Outliner.

17
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.579
And this is where we can manage our content that's

18
00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:44.200
in there and our assemblies.

19
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.439
The middle section is our timeline.

20
00:00:47.439 --> 00:00:48.920
So this allows us actually scrub

21
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.719
through the various different frames of our actual sequence

22
00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:56.520
to be able to see and play back everything very traditional

23
00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.679
or very similarly related to traditional nonlinear editing.

24
00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:02.579
We can actually just scrub through the timeline

25
00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:04.099
and see everything.

26
00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.620
And then at the bottom, we have our playback control.

27
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.859
So this is going to be our time slider, our range

28
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:11.060
slider, which can allow us to kind of zoom in

29
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.480
on portions of our timeline and see it.

30
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.260
And of course, we can play, reverse, go to the front,

31
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.019
go to the back, et cetera.

32
00:01:18.019 --> 00:01:21.140
So let's go ahead and jump over to the engine.

33
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.260
Now I've got our map open.

34
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.219
So this is our sequence 905.

35
00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:29.140
And within our content browser, under sequence 105,

36
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.219
maps is the sequence 105 game dev, which is open.

37
00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:36.099
Now I already have the sequence that is open here.

38
00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:37.540
So just to show you real quick,

39
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.299
so I'm going to close out Sequencer.

40
00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:41.180
So see, it closes the sequence.

41
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.180
And if I were to go up to Window,

42
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.620
and what we're going to look for is underneath Cinematics,

43
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.060
Sequencer, if I open this, it will appear

44
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.219
like the tool is broken.

45
00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:50.099
It's not.

46
00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:51.560
Again, what we need to do is we

47
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.420
need to actually load a sequence.

48
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.920
So from within the engine itself,

49
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.140
or at least within this map, you

50
00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.879
can see just this little dropdown here,

51
00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:01.579
which is our level sequences.

52
00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:03.780
There is this MSQ 101.

53
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.579
And I could click this, and it should open my sequence.

54
00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:09.300
You'll also notice, because we have it within our world,

55
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.939
it's also available over here in our Outliner.

56
00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:13.460
So by simply clicking it, you'll

57
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.080
see there is a open level sequence within the Details

58
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:16.860
panel.

59
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.259
Either method that you use will get you the same results.

60
00:02:20.259 --> 00:02:21.860
So down here, inside of our sequence,

61
00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.099
now we get to see all of our controls.

62
00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:26.659
Now, if you've opened this and you scrub this back,

63
00:02:26.699 --> 00:02:28.979
you'll notice that the camera is decoupled.

64
00:02:28.979 --> 00:02:31.300
All we simply need to do is in our Camera Cuts

65
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.039
track within the sequence, click the little Camera

66
00:02:34.039 --> 00:02:37.430
button, and it will lock the camera to your viewport.

67
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.469
So you can see now as we scrub through,

68
00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:43.110
we'll actually see our entire sequence play out.

69
00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.389
All right, so to cover just the Sequencer

70
00:02:46.389 --> 00:02:50.349
UI that's available, so we've got Save, we've got Open,

71
00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:53.030
we've got a couple of tools as well to create cameras

72
00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.569
or even export.

73
00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.250
Across the middle section here, we've

74
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.409
got various different actions that we can do.

75
00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:02.599
These are worth looking into.

76
00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:05.280
We also have View Capability, so you can see here

77
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.719
we can actually turn and off different features.

78
00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:09.599
These are really quality of life

79
00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:13.240
that allows you to work faster or have better recognition

80
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.280
and readability on your sequence.

81
00:03:16.139 --> 00:03:18.360
Keep moving across here, you can see as well we have some

82
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.300
playback options as well.

83
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.580
A couple really handy key features

84
00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.060
that I definitely recommend when it comes to working

85
00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:28.900
with Sequencer is this concept of that we're

86
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.379
going to be keying certain actions or certain attributes

87
00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.539
on our actor.

88
00:03:34.539 --> 00:03:38.050
So for example, if I select our little purple guy here,

89
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.889
and I'll scroll down, you'll notice

90
00:03:39.889 --> 00:03:42.289
that inside of the transform here,

91
00:03:42.289 --> 00:03:43.889
we have various different keys.

92
00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:45.370
I'll highlight those real quick.

93
00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.689
And those keys are just traditional animation principles

94
00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:52.449
where we record basically a value on our timeline

95
00:03:52.449 --> 00:03:56.129
that says, hey, when the playback range reaches

96
00:03:56.129 --> 00:04:01.319
this specific spot, I want these values to be present.

97
00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:03.580
So with this, whenever we create key frames,

98
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.560
we can do it one of two ways.

99
00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.479
We can create it manually each and every time.

100
00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:09.960
So for example, if I move my character, I'll go in here

101
00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.199
and I'll click my little plus button to set a key.

102
00:04:12.199 --> 00:04:15.280
I could do that every time I wanted to change a property,

103
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.819
or we can work just a little bit smarter

104
00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:21.639
or make our lives a little bit easier by using Auto Key.

105
00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:24.720
So when this is turned on, I'll highlight over this

106
00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:27.240
that says, creates a key when channels or properties change.

107
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.720
Now something important to note is

108
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.600
that only automatically adds a key when there's already

109
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.850
a track and at least one key.

110
00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.370
So a good typical workflow here is

111
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.730
I would take my character underneath my transforms.

112
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.009
I can hit this plus button as opposed

113
00:04:44.009 --> 00:04:46.529
to the plus button on the individual channels,

114
00:04:46.529 --> 00:04:49.449
and it will create a key for all of my properties.

115
00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:51.290
In fact, you can see that right here.

116
00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.209
Let me kind of zoom in a little bit.

117
00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.290
So you can see that we have these three keys

118
00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.889
for our location, rotation, and scale.

119
00:04:57.889 --> 00:04:59.889
And then we can continue to build out our shot

120
00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:01.089
with Auto Key turned on.

121
00:05:01.089 --> 00:05:03.329
And as I move my playback range,

122
00:05:03.329 --> 00:05:05.370
you'll notice that as I move the player

123
00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.449
inside of the viewport,

124
00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:09.089
it'll actually just create a key frame

125
00:05:09.089 --> 00:05:10.850
for only the changed properties.

126
00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.250
Now again, in order for Auto Keyframe to work,

127
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.379
you have to have some key frames present.

128
00:05:16.379 --> 00:05:17.420
So if you notice from our keys,

129
00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.720
we are only changing location,

130
00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.860
we're not rotating or scaling,

131
00:05:21.899 --> 00:05:25.019
and the engine's automatically created a key frame for us

132
00:05:25.019 --> 00:05:27.939
by using this Auto Keyframe.

133
00:05:27.939 --> 00:05:29.399
All right, so now that it's understood,

134
00:05:29.399 --> 00:05:30.939
you'll see here from this dropdown,

135
00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:32.759
key change, the default behavior,

136
00:05:32.759 --> 00:05:36.209
key group, key all, are some other things.

137
00:05:36.209 --> 00:05:37.410
And then without going too deep

138
00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.930
on key frame interpolation,

139
00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.610
you'll see that we can specify

140
00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.269
whatever the default key is.

141
00:05:44.269 --> 00:05:46.569
All right, so you can see this is a great feature.

142
00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:48.769
So this is allows all edits,

143
00:05:48.769 --> 00:05:51.730
allow sequencer edits only, allow level edits.

144
00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:54.209
If you recall in the previous video,

145
00:05:54.209 --> 00:05:58.610
we talked about how we can have possessables and spawnables.

146
00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.790
So as we're animating our content

147
00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.170
or whatever it is inside of our sequence,

148
00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.449
you'll see that we can actually make edits

149
00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:09.329
both in the world or in the sequence

150
00:06:09.329 --> 00:06:11.339
or in the world separate.

151
00:06:11.339 --> 00:06:12.819
So this is very handy.

152
00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:15.500
There are special cases where you do want to use this,

153
00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.779
but by using allow all edits,

154
00:06:17.779 --> 00:06:20.180
you should be completely fine.

155
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.620
And then finally we have our snapping.

156
00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.180
So this is gonna allow us to snap our key frames,

157
00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.160
also allow us to snap our grid.

158
00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.959
So this is very handy.

159
00:06:29.959 --> 00:06:30.939
Playback range here.

160
00:06:30.939 --> 00:06:33.019
So something that's very important to note here

161
00:06:33.019 --> 00:06:36.579
is by changing your FPS,

162
00:06:36.579 --> 00:06:40.220
you're not effectively changing the overall compression

163
00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:41.579
or your final output.

164
00:06:41.579 --> 00:06:43.800
Because we're working within a digital medium,

165
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.339
we're really not confined to a,

166
00:06:46.339 --> 00:06:49.500
this is your FPS if that makes sense.

167
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.420
But instead this is going to be your playback speed.

168
00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.660
You can dynamically change this at any point.

169
00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.660
However, the biggest recommendation I have

170
00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:01.660
is determine what your playback rate is going to be prior

171
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.319
and try to just stick that

172
00:07:03.319 --> 00:07:04.759
throughout the entire production.

173
00:07:04.759 --> 00:07:07.379
Now, traditionally in game development,

174
00:07:07.379 --> 00:07:09.480
we use 30 frames per second.

175
00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.100
It feels cinematic.

176
00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.459
It's easier on the player.

177
00:07:12.459 --> 00:07:14.779
And as we start pumping more content,

178
00:07:14.779 --> 00:07:17.100
obviously the higher FPS that you go

179
00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.220
requires more hardware resources.

180
00:07:19.779 --> 00:07:21.300
So using something like 30 frames per second

181
00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.860
is absolutely a safe bet

182
00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.230
when it comes to game development.

183
00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.509
One of the other tools kind of bridging off

184
00:07:28.509 --> 00:07:29.949
of this concept we're talking about

185
00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:33.829
with animation key frames and creating these

186
00:07:33.829 --> 00:07:36.750
is at any point, so I'm just gonna select my transform.

187
00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.990
You can open up the curve editor

188
00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:41.810
and the curve editor is going to show you

189
00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.189
all of those key frames.

190
00:07:43.189 --> 00:07:45.810
Now in the case of this example, I have the bot lock.

191
00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.389
That's why I can't select the key frames and move those.

192
00:07:48.389 --> 00:07:50.490
So I don't accidentally change anything,

193
00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.269
but if it were unlocked, you could see here

194
00:07:52.269 --> 00:07:54.149
that I could go and select the key frames,

195
00:07:54.149 --> 00:07:56.110
move them, manipulate them.

196
00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:59.250
Very traditional animation to be able to get the look,

197
00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.589
the feel, the movement, the timing

198
00:08:01.589 --> 00:08:02.629
that you're looking for.

199
00:08:02.629 --> 00:08:06.129
And we can do that through the curve editor.

200
00:08:06.129 --> 00:08:07.829
So moving on to the second section.

201
00:08:07.829 --> 00:08:10.189
In fact, we've already kind of been working with it.

202
00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:11.230
You'll see that this is essentially

203
00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:13.529
just our content assembly.

204
00:08:13.529 --> 00:08:17.209
Now, inside of this sequence that we're using,

205
00:08:17.209 --> 00:08:19.490
you'll see that we've got various different folders.

206
00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:21.569
We have some cinema cameras.

207
00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:23.089
As you saw in the previous example,

208
00:08:23.089 --> 00:08:27.019
we're obviously manipulating the little bot that's in here.

209
00:08:27.019 --> 00:08:29.300
I've also organized some various lighting

210
00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.220
that we're using, our events,

211
00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.980
our triggers that we have here.

212
00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:35.899
So essentially this is just our assembly area,

213
00:08:35.899 --> 00:08:38.259
very similar to our outliner.

214
00:08:38.259 --> 00:08:40.960
But in this case, we're only seeing the content

215
00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.379
that is being controlled, manipulated or displayed

216
00:08:44.419 --> 00:08:47.639
by Sequencer, not our actual world content,

217
00:08:47.639 --> 00:08:50.139
which is still available over here.

218
00:08:50.139 --> 00:08:51.940
So we talked about the curves editor,

219
00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.559
which is a great way to refine your motion,

220
00:08:54.559 --> 00:08:56.779
your animation, and your timing.

221
00:08:56.779 --> 00:08:59.779
One of the other things that we will actually be building

222
00:08:59.779 --> 00:09:03.059
in a later course is the events manager.

223
00:09:03.059 --> 00:09:06.379
So a very cool and very handy feature of Sequencer

224
00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:10.600
is that it allows us to essentially cross talk

225
00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.980
between our sequence and the world itself.

226
00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:16.220
So we can do things like firing off events.

227
00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.860
We can trigger certain elements as well.

228
00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.740
For the purposes of this class and what we're building

229
00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.460
and then the subsequent class that's after this,

230
00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:28.179
we'll actually be using events to target

231
00:09:28.179 --> 00:09:29.700
an events manager.

232
00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.379
The whole principle here is that we're building

233
00:09:31.379 --> 00:09:32.580
a quick cut scene.

234
00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:34.500
And at some point during our sequence,

235
00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.620
we want the player to invoke some kind of action.

236
00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.100
When this case is just, hey, we need you to press

237
00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.820
a button to confirm that you acknowledge

238
00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.240
what the NPC wants you to do.

239
00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:46.519
So we can do that by using events.

240
00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:48.259
So that way we're building our world smarter.

241
00:09:48.259 --> 00:09:52.330
So we have a blueprint that essentially contains

242
00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.570
the logic for our player controls to pause our sequence

243
00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.450
to, okay, the player chose this action, go do this,

244
00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:00.809
which exists outside of our sequence.

245
00:10:00.809 --> 00:10:04.830
So by using the events feature within sequence,

246
00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:07.169
we can actually target that, fire off different commands

247
00:10:07.169 --> 00:10:10.210
back and forth, and it won't really give this player

248
00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:13.289
this feeling of they're in control of the narrative,

249
00:10:13.289 --> 00:10:16.169
all while using Sequencer.

250
00:10:16.169 --> 00:10:18.129
Something else to keep in mind is that

251
00:10:18.129 --> 00:10:20.549
Unreal ships with a lot of pre-built templates.

252
00:10:20.549 --> 00:10:22.970
And these are great for getting you started.

253
00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.649
But one thing that's very key to understanding

254
00:10:25.649 --> 00:10:27.389
about the way these templates work

255
00:10:27.389 --> 00:10:29.289
and how you can use them.

256
00:10:29.289 --> 00:10:32.549
So templates aren't a special version of the engine, right?

257
00:10:32.549 --> 00:10:36.730
So if I use the games template and I use first person

258
00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.269
versus, oh no, I need to create a third person.

259
00:10:39.269 --> 00:10:41.389
It's important to note that it's the exact same engine.

260
00:10:41.389 --> 00:10:44.990
In fact, every single one of our project templates

261
00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.190
uses the same engine.

262
00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.470
So the question is, well, why use a template?

263
00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.509
Well, templates do fundamentally two things.

264
00:10:52.509 --> 00:10:55.230
So the first thing is that it will actually include

265
00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.990
this content for you when you create your project.

266
00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.029
So example, if we were using a first person shooter,

267
00:11:01.029 --> 00:11:04.909
obviously we would want to have the arms and a weapon rig

268
00:11:04.909 --> 00:11:06.549
and we'd wanna have a player camera

269
00:11:06.549 --> 00:11:10.509
that when you possess will appear as a first person, right?

270
00:11:10.509 --> 00:11:13.070
Now that's gonna be different from a third person

271
00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.330
or even a top down.

272
00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:16.970
So when you create your template,

273
00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:19.870
the engine is gonna automatically give you this content

274
00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:21.730
within its own special folder.

275
00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:24.649
So typically you'll see something like TP underscore

276
00:11:24.649 --> 00:11:27.850
first person, TP underscore third person,

277
00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:31.350
or something that's its own standalone folder

278
00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:32.690
within your project.

279
00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.909
All right, so that's the first principle

280
00:11:34.909 --> 00:11:36.529
about understanding templates.

281
00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:38.190
The second thing is that templates

282
00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:42.289
will automatically flag various different features

283
00:11:42.289 --> 00:11:43.210
within the project.

284
00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.389
So if you recall, underneath our plugins,

285
00:11:45.389 --> 00:11:47.690
we could go in and we can enable certain things.

286
00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:51.509
Now, for example, let's say that we're creating

287
00:11:51.509 --> 00:11:53.970
a blank project, but it wants to,

288
00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:55.870
we wanna use like an open world, right?

289
00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:58.649
There are a few features or a few pieces of content

290
00:11:58.649 --> 00:12:01.889
or plugins that the engine typically would use

291
00:12:01.889 --> 00:12:03.129
in that process.

292
00:12:03.129 --> 00:12:04.549
So when you choose that template,

293
00:12:04.549 --> 00:12:07.269
the engine is going to drop in some example content

294
00:12:07.269 --> 00:12:09.870
for you, and then it's also going to enable

295
00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.769
those plugins that you need.

296
00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:13.669
As a counterpoint, now I know we're talking

297
00:12:13.669 --> 00:12:16.710
about game development, but when it comes to automotive,

298
00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:19.629
product design and manufacturing, typically speaking,

299
00:12:19.629 --> 00:12:22.230
you're looking for the highest quality rendering.

300
00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.830
You're not looking for ultra high frames per second,

301
00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:27.350
but rather the highest visual quality.

302
00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.389
So by using the automotive product design

303
00:12:29.389 --> 00:12:31.789
and manufacturing template, the engine is going to

304
00:12:31.789 --> 00:12:33.789
go ahead and change a few settings in the project

305
00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.830
to ramp up the visuals, which will obviously degrade

306
00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:39.110
our performance, but again, that's intended.

307
00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:42.590
We want better visuals over high frames per second.

308
00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:45.250
It will enable certain plugins, and then it will even drop

309
00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:48.629
in some example content of a pre-built scene specifically

310
00:12:48.629 --> 00:12:51.789
for that automotive and product design and manufacturing.

311
00:12:51.789 --> 00:12:53.740
So hopefully that makes sense.

312
00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.639
At any point, you can always go back

313
00:12:55.639 --> 00:12:58.179
and remove content from a template.

314
00:12:58.179 --> 00:13:01.679
You can copy that template over to a new project.

315
00:13:01.679 --> 00:13:05.240
You can even go in and disable the plugins altogether

316
00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:06.799
that you don't need anymore.

317
00:13:06.799 --> 00:13:09.000
So just know that when you start with a template,

318
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.480
it doesn't mean that you're stuck.

319
00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.539
You can't change it, you can't alter it.

320
00:13:12.539 --> 00:13:14.700
At any point, you absolutely can.

321
00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.879
Now, another cool feature on the whole subject

322
00:13:16.879 --> 00:13:19.879
of templates is we can actually create

323
00:13:19.879 --> 00:13:21.799
a templated sequence.

324
00:13:21.799 --> 00:13:24.259
Now, this is a more advanced topic.

325
00:13:24.259 --> 00:13:26.360
We're going to be creating a sequence from scratch

326
00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:29.279
so we kind of understand the process of building it,

327
00:13:29.279 --> 00:13:31.480
but a really cool feature that's been in the engine

328
00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.919
for quite a while is on the main toolbar

329
00:13:34.919 --> 00:13:37.600
underneath our cinematics, we have this add level sequence

330
00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.070
with shots.

331
00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:42.590
And that's going to open up this sequence or this master

332
00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:46.549
sequence shot settings in which we can allow the engine

333
00:13:46.549 --> 00:13:51.429
to pre-allocate and pre-build various different folder

334
00:13:51.429 --> 00:13:53.710
structures, the naming conventions

335
00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:57.379
we have for our shots, for our subsequences, et cetera.

336
00:13:57.379 --> 00:13:58.899
My best recommendation rather than

337
00:13:58.899 --> 00:14:01.580
us going through these settings is to play with this.

338
00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.059
Now, it's important, go ahead and set your base path

339
00:14:04.059 --> 00:14:06.740
if you are in an active project to a folder that's

340
00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:08.899
not going to touch any of your existing content

341
00:14:08.899 --> 00:14:11.259
so you can see what's actually occurring.

342
00:14:11.259 --> 00:14:13.299
So in this case, this is the default,

343
00:14:13.299 --> 00:14:14.539
just to highlight real quick.

344
00:14:14.539 --> 00:14:17.820
So it will give name sequence, suffix of root.

345
00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.940
You'll see that it's created five shots.

346
00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:22.379
Now, if I skip down to this little screenshot here,

347
00:14:22.379 --> 00:14:25.580
you'll see we have our five shots established.

348
00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.259
And they have the same length,

349
00:14:27.259 --> 00:14:30.379
which is going to be our five seconds here.

350
00:14:30.379 --> 00:14:32.899
We can do shot directories, first shot numbers,

351
00:14:32.899 --> 00:14:33.860
takes, et cetera.

352
00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:37.580
This is very handy if you want to get a master sequence set up

353
00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:40.620
very, very quickly with it already pre-populated.

354
00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:43.460
So I highly recommend, again, on the main toolbar,

355
00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:46.620
click the little film snap box here.

356
00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.899
And you'll see there's an add level sequence with shots.

357
00:14:48.899 --> 00:14:50.860
Go ahead and create a level sequence with shots.

358
00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:52.779
And just take a look at how that is set up.
